The baseline is already minimal: one plaintext add, no multiplications, rotations, rescales or levels. Any rewrite would either add operations or normalize to the same circuit, so I don't expect this kernel to beat the baseline. I'm resubmitting it unchanged.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext s("s");
  Ciphertext y = x0 + s;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

A circuit with fewer than one operation can't produce `x0 + s`, so I don't see a cheaper circuit. The only alternatives add operations. Here I try the operand-swapped form `s + x0`, which may lower differently in CHEHAB. If it normalizes to the same circuit, it will again be rejected as identical.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext s("s");
  Ciphertext y = s + x0;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline is already at the minimum for this task: one add, with no multiplications, rotations, rescales or levels. Reordering the operands (attempt 2) produced the same circuit, and any structurally different circuit would need extra operations and run slower.

I have no kernel that is both different and cheaper, so I'm keeping the baseline form. Spending the remaining attempts on slower variants would not help.

